  {/* ---------------- OVERVIEW ---------------- */}
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    MatchRoom is a social second-screen app for live sports, built solo over a
    hackathon weekend for <strong>Hacks United v7</strong>. You join a room
    with friends, pick a side, and react as the game unfolds. An AI
    &ldquo;producer&rdquo; watches the live match feed and invents
    micro-predictions at tense moments &mdash; <em>&ldquo;Mbapp&eacute; steps
    up. Does he bury this penalty?&rdquo;</em> &mdash; with a countdown to lock
    in your pick. Live chat, faction support, points, streaks, receipt
    battles, and room leaderboards turn the broadcast into a shared
    competition, synchronized to the same clock across every browser in the
    room.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/matchroom/banner.svg"
      alt="MatchRoom banner: 'Watch together. Compete together.'"
    />
  </figure>

  {/* ---------------- WHY ---------------- */}
  <h2>Why I built it</h2>
  <p>
    Not everyone who cares about a match can be in the stadium, at a watch
    party, or even in the same city as the friends they&rsquo;d normally watch
    with. But they should still get to <em>feel</em> like part of the crowd
    &mdash; the noise, the tension, the shared willing-it-forward &mdash; not
    just watch the same score tick over on a screen somewhere else. That gap
    is what I built MatchRoom to close: a room a scattered group of
    supporters can share that feels less like a livestream and more like
    standing in the stands together.
  </p>
  <p>
    The seed of it was smaller and more familiar: every big match, my group
    chat turns into chaos. Someone types &ldquo;he&rsquo;s going to
    miss&rdquo; and the text lands after the ball is already in the net. The
    energy is real, but it&rsquo;s disconnected from the game &mdash; a
    message thread can&rsquo;t capture what it feels like to stand among
    supporters who are celebrating, arguing, and willing their side forward.
    I wanted to build the room <em>around</em> the match instead of next to
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the whole product is built for the passionate-supporter feeling first:
    you pick a side the moment you walk in, not a neutral prediction-market
    stance but a <strong>faction</strong> you&rsquo;re rooting for. Every
    mechanic &mdash; the countdown polls, the reactions, the streaks, the
    leaderboard &mdash; exists to give that rooting interest somewhere to go.
    Calling the next moment before it happens, celebrating a correct pick
    with your faction, needling the other side in chat &mdash; that&rsquo;s
    fandom, gamified, not a betting slip. Synchronized to the match clock,
    with your friends, wherever they physically are, is what actually
    delivers the &ldquo;together&rdquo; part.
  </p>
  <p>
    And I wanted the AI part to be genuinely load-bearing in service of that,
    not a chatbot bolted on: an AI{" "}
    <em>producer</em> that watches the event feed and invents the moments a
    room gets to react to on the fly, and an AI <em>referee</em> that turns
    dropped news links into competitive currency for the debate rooms, so
    supporters who are more into the discourse than a live match still have a
    faction to fight for.
  </p>

