  {/* ---------------- HOW IT WORKS ---------------- */}
  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    The architectural principle everything hangs off: <strong>the LLM
    authors, the engine settles.</strong> A language model never decides who
    wins anything &mdash; it only writes verifiable questions that a
    deterministic engine resolves.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      The <strong>ReplayEngine</strong> (<code>src/lib/replay/engine.ts</code>)
      ticks a real match timeline out on a clock, emitting structured{" "}
      <code>MatchEvent</code>s &mdash; the demo replays the 2022 World Cup
      final. A production feed can replace this source without changing
      anything downstream.
    </li>
    <li>
      On interesting events (penalty, dangerous free kick, late one-goal
      game), the client calls <code>POST /api/producer</code>. The producer
      LLM sees a rolling window of recent events and either declines or
      returns a prediction card as strict JSON &mdash; question, two options,
      pick window, and a <strong>resolution rule</strong> (&ldquo;resolves
      YES if a <code>penalty_result</code> event with{" "}
      <code>outcome=scored</code> arrives within 120s&rdquo;).
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://github.com/phamhscott/matchroom/blob/main/src/lib/producer/schema.ts">Zod validates </a>
      the generation; malformed output is dropped, and can&rsquo;t-miss
      moments fall back to hardcoded safety-net cards.
    </li>
    <li>
      The card is written to Supabase; <strong>Realtime</strong> broadcasts it
      to everyone in the room. Picks stream in live with a countdown.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>AI personas</strong> (homer / stat-nerd / chaos agent) pick a
      side via deterministic bias and drop one line of in-character trash talk
      via a fast LLM call &mdash; so an API hiccup never freezes a pick, only
      the flavor text.
    </li>
    <li>
      When the resolving event arrives in the stream, the engine settles the
      card, points burst, and the leaderboard reshuffles.
    </li>
    <li>
      In debate rooms, <code>POST /api/grade-source</code> safely extracts a
      submitted page and asks an LLM referee for relevance, credibility, and
      camp-support scores. The server validates those scores, computes the
      payout itself, blocks duplicate URL credit, and publishes the receipt
      through the same reaction stream everything else uses.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The Analyst</strong> (<code>POST /api/debater</code>) retrieves
      text from the room&rsquo;s starter and user-submitted sources, combines
      it with recent chat, and argues for the opposing camp using
      room-specific evidence &mdash; grounded generation over a room-sized
      corpus, no vector store needed.
    </li>
  </ol>

  {/* ---------------- TECHNICAL HIGHLIGHTS ---------------- */}
  <h2>Technical highlights</h2>

  <h3>LLM output that cannot control game results</h3>
  <p>
    The producer uses a heuristic pre-filter to avoid unnecessary calls, sends
    a rolling event window through a provider-independent LLM layer (Anthropic
    / Groq / xAI / Gemini, in environment-key priority order), and requests a
    strict prediction schema. The model authors questions and machine-readable
    rules, but only the event engine determines winners. Priority queues,
    deterministic fallback cards, request timeouts, and id-based
    deduplication keep the live room responsive during latency, malformed
    output, and concurrent clients.
  </p>

  <h3>Synchronized multiplayer replay</h3>
  <p>
    Every shared room stores a replay epoch and speed in Postgres. On join,{" "}
    <code>useRoom</code> compares server and client timestamps, silently seeks
    through elapsed events, and advances from the shared wall clock. Supabase
    Realtime broadcasts cards, picks, reactions, member updates, and
    room-wide replay resets &mdash; that&rsquo;s the entire multiplayer layer,
    no custom websocket code. Late joiners restore a persisted snapshot before
    subscribing.
  </p>

  <h3>Competitive integrity and graceful degradation</h3>
  <p>
    Faction scoring separates expression from competition: unlimited chat
    scores nothing, each member can contribute crowd energy only once per
    moment, and conviction is a limited resource spent when a pick is
    submitted. Minority-pick bonuses reward correct contrarian calls without
    handing out free comeback points. The same pure award function runs
    locally and on the resolution route. Without Supabase or an LLM key, the
    app still runs in a solo mode with local scoring and deterministic
    fallbacks &mdash; every external dependency has a rehearsed failure mode.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- CHALLENGES ---------------- */}
  <h2>Challenges worth remembering</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The spoiler problem.</strong> Player profiles originally
    aggregated stats from the full match timeline &mdash; so clicking a
    player at kickoff already showed his final goal tally. In a
    replay-as-live product that&rsquo;s fatal. I rebuilt player profiles to
    seed rosters from lineups but aggregate stats <em>only from events already
    revealed by the clock</em>, so the Players tab updates live with the feed
    and never leaks the future.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>LLM latency versus a live clock.</strong> A prediction card that
    arrives after the penalty is worthless. Producer and persona calls run
    under tight timeouts (~1.5s) with heuristic pre-filters and deterministic
    fallbacks; slower calls that aren&rsquo;t on the replay clock (source
    grading, the Analyst) get a separate 8s budget. Designing every AI
    feature so its failure mode is <em>silence or a fallback, never
    breakage</em> was the single most repeated lesson of the weekend.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Realtime consistency.</strong> Late joiners need history plus
    live updates without duplicates, and your own writes echo back through
    the subscription. I ended up with id-based deduplication everywhere,
    bootstrap-then-subscribe ordering, and a rule that payouts to{" "}
    <em>yourself</em> apply from the API response while everyone else&rsquo;s
    arrive via Realtime.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Synchronized minigames without a server tick.</strong> The
    Receipt Battle minigame needed every browser to agree on when a
    90-second window ends and who won, without adding server-side
    resolution. The trick: the battle marker carries its end time{" "}
    <em>in replay-clock seconds</em>, and every client computes the same
    per-camp totals from the same graded receipts inside the window.
    Deterministic inputs produce identical outcomes on every screen.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Live data temptation.</strong> I investigated intercepting a
    commercial live-scores API and decided against it &mdash; signed headers,
    ToS, brittle. The replay engine emits the same <code>MatchEvent</code>{" "}
    contract a real provider adapter would, so swapping in live data is a
    drop-in change rather than a rewrite.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- SCOPE / HONEST NOTE ---------------- */}
  <h2>What shipped, and what's next</h2>
  <p>
    This was solo, built in a hackathon weekend &mdash; so the scope is
    deliberately a working prototype, not a production app. There&rsquo;s no
    authentication (members are bound by a local profile, not an account), no
    rate limiting on the LLM-backed routes, and scoring runs through
    application code rather than atomic Postgres functions. The replay engine
    streams a recorded match rather than a live feed, though it emits the
    same <code>MatchEvent</code> contract a real provider adapter would need,
    so that&rsquo;s meant to be a drop-in swap rather than a rewrite.
  </p>
  <p>
    Next up, roughly in priority order: real live fixtures behind the
    existing event adapter, authentication binding members to accounts, rate
    limits and atomic scoring functions, persisted custom rooms with invites,
    and more sports &mdash; the event contract and resolution rules
    generalize well beyond football.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- REFLECTION ---------------- */}
  <h2>What I took away</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Determinism is the product.</strong> Every &ldquo;wow&rdquo;
      moment (the AI invents a prediction, the referee pays your receipt) is
      trustworthy only because a boring deterministic layer settles it.
      Splitting authorship from settlement made hallucination structurally
      impossible.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Design the failure mode first.</strong> Timeouts, fallback
      cards, fallback grades, canned Analyst lines &mdash; deciding what
      happens when the LLM <em>doesn&rsquo;t</em> answer shaped the
      architecture more than the happy path did.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Transport reuse beats new infrastructure.</strong> Encoding new
      message kinds into one existing Realtime stream let me ship a referee, a
      grounded debater, and a synced minigame in a weekend without a single
      migration.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Incentive design is real design.</strong>{" "}
      &ldquo;Chat scores nothing, unique reactions score once, conviction is
      scarce, contrarians get paid&rdquo; took as much iteration as any code
      &mdash; and it&rsquo;s what makes the room feel competitive instead of
      spammy.
    </li>
  </ul>
